---
// Colophon (/colophon/) — specs/pages/colophon.md. Footer-linked only, never
// in primary nav (§2 row 9 of specs/routes/sitemap.md): a reward for the
// curious, not a headline claim. Deep-dive editorial archetype.
//
// The register test is this page's reason to exist (§2.1): every claim is
// paired with what it cost or what it replaced. A sentence that only says
// what was used does not belong, and no technology gets promotional framing —
// including the ones this project chose (CLAUDE.md). Apply the test on every
// edit: read each sentence and ask what it concedes.
//
// Not built, by §2.4: live build/deploy status, CI badges, an architecture
// diagram nobody will redraw, performance scores. The first two would make a
// static page depend on the API and invert the R4 anchor, which is why the
// absence is structural rather than a matter of taste.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY MISSING, AND WHEN IT LANDS (§3, §4): the page
// describes what is running. A sentence in the future tense here is a bug.
// So the dynamic layer (endpoints are stubs, no database is deployed),
// containers and deploy (nothing is provisioned), and the monthly cost
// (nothing is billed yet) have no sections. Each gets one when it runs — not
// before, and the review date below moves with them.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { repo } from '../lib/links';
import '../styles/article.css';
import '../styles/reference.css';

// A review date, not a build date (§2.2): a build timestamp would change on
// every deploy without anyone checking anything, and would assert a freshness
// the words do not have. Month precision. Moving this is what "reviewed"
// means — bump it when the page has actually been read against reality.
const reviewed = '2026-08';
---

<Base
  title="colophon · T://bendet"
  description="What runs this site and what each choice cost: static Astro, MDX content in the repository, a Hebrew locale subtree, and a hand-written pipeline."
  section="colophon"
>
  <header class="article-header">
    <h1>colophon</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      What runs this site, and what each choice cost. Every claim below is paired with what
      it gave up.
    </p>
    <p class="reviewed">
      reviewed <time datetime={reviewed}>{reviewed}</time>
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>the static core</h2>
    <div class="article-body">
      <p>
        Astro 7 with static output and no adapter: every public page is HTML on disk before
        anyone asks for it, and the reading path carries no framework JavaScript.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cost is paid at both ends. Publishing is a build and a deploy, so correcting a
        typo is a pipeline run rather than an edit. And nothing that differs per visitor can
        live here at all — it has to be a second system with its own uptime, which is a
        larger bill than server-rendering the site would have been.
      </p>
      <p>
        Content sits in typed collections, so a missing field fails the build instead of the
        page. That trade buys a schema to migrate: adding a field means touching every entry
        written before it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>content and language</h2>
    <div class="article-body">
      <p>
        Articles and translations are MDX files in the repository, versioned alongside the
        code that renders them. The cost is that publishing requires git — there is no
        editor, nothing can be written from a phone, and every correction is a commit
        anyone can read.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hebrew is a prebuilt locale subtree at <code>/he/</code> with <code>dir="rtl"</code>,
        carrying its own index, its own feed, and its own routes rather than a
        machine-translated mirror of the English ones.
      </p>
      <p>
        Localization stops there, on purpose: the home page, projects, and this site's about
        page have no Hebrew counterpart, so a Hebrew reader who follows a link out of the
        translations gets English. Translating the chrome around a body of mostly English
        writing would advertise a bilingual site that does not exist. A visible seam is
        easier to read than a half-kept promise.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>the pipeline</h2>
    <div class="article-body">
      <p>
        One workflow runs on every pull request: typecheck across both packages, the API's
        tests against a Postgres service container with migrations applied first, then the
        site build and both container images.
      </p>
      <p>
        "From scratch" is a narrow claim, and worth stating narrowly. The vendor supplies
        the workflow syntax and two primitive actions — a checkout and a Node setup. Every
        stage's logic above that is authored here. What that costs is the debugging a
        marketplace action would have absorbed: a hand-written stage that breaks on a runner
        image update is a hand-written stage someone has to read.
      </p>
      <p>
        Third-party actions are blocked at the repository level and anything that runs must
        be pinned to a commit. It is a real constraint rather than a stance: adding one is a
        settings change before it is a workflow change, and the failure when you forget is
        an opaque permissions error.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>the record</h2>
    <div class="article-body">
      <p>
        The site is built from a public repository, and the decisions behind it were argued
        somewhere rather than asserted here — including the ones rejected and the ones
        reversed. The specs are the distilled result; the argument itself is in the history
        before them. They are working documents, not visitor documents, and this page does
        not depend on anyone reading them.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="ref-rows pointers">
      <li class="ref-row">
        <span class="ref-label">repository</span>
        <span class="ref-value">
          <a href={repo} rel="noopener">github.com/t-bendet/portfolio-project</a>
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="ref-row">
        <span class="ref-label">decisions</span>
        <span class="ref-value">
          <code>specs/</code> in the repository, and the commit history behind it
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>

